{"name":"Zebra Finch","hex":"#cec6bb","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zebra finch","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small Australasian weaverbird of the genus Taeniopygia that has markings similar to a zebra."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zebra_finch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zebra finch","blocks":[{"text":"The zebra finches are two species of estrildid finch in the genus Taeniopygia found in Australia and Indonesia. They are seed-eaters that travel in large flocks.\nThey typically live for 2 to 3 years in the wild."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Zebra_finch_group.png/330px-Zebra_finch_group.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zebra_finch","wikidata":"Q3214630"},{"title":"Zebra","blocks":[{"text":"Zebras are African equines with distinctive black-and-white striped coats. There are three living species: Grévy's zebra, the plains zebra, and the mountain zebra. Zebras share the genus Equus with horses and asses, the three groups being the only living members of the family Equidae. Zebra stripes come in different patterns, unique to each individual. Several theories have been proposed for the function of these patterns, with most evidence supporting them as a deterrent for biting flies. Zebras inhabit eastern and southern Africa and can be found in a variety of habitats such as savannahs, grasslands, woodlands, shrublands, and mountainous areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Plains_Zebra_Equus_quagga_cropped.jpg/330px-Plains_Zebra_Equus_quagga_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zebra","wikidata":"Q32789"},{"title":"Finch","blocks":[{"text":"The true finches are small to medium-sized passerine birds in the family Fringillidae. Finches generally have stout conical bills adapted for eating seeds and nuts and often have colourful plumage. They occupy a great range of habitats where they are usually resident and do not migrate. They have a worldwide native distribution except for Australia and the polar regions. The family Fringillidae contains more than two hundred species divided into fifty genera. It includes the canaries, siskins, redpolls, serins, grosbeaks and euphonias, as well as the morphologically divergent Hawaiian honeycreepers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Pyrrhula_pyrrhula_female_2.jpg/330px-Pyrrhula_pyrrhula_female_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finch","wikidata":"Q160835"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/2014-08-19_Zebra_Finch%2C_Sumba%2C_Nusa_Tenggara_Timur%2C_Indonesia_1.jpg/500px-2014-08-19_Zebra_Finch%2C_Sumba%2C_Nusa_Tenggara_Timur%2C_Indonesia_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/2014-08-19_Zebra_Finch%2C_Sumba%2C_Nusa_Tenggara_Timur%2C_Indonesia_1.jpg/500px-2014-08-19_Zebra_Finch%2C_Sumba%2C_Nusa_Tenggara_Timur%2C_Indonesia_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2014-08-19_Zebra_Finch%2C_Sumba%2C_Nusa_Tenggara_Timur%2C_Indonesia_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Australian_zebra_finch_Chestnut-eared_Finch_%28Taeniopygia_castanotis%29_pair.jpg/500px-Australian_zebra_finch_Chestnut-eared_Finch_%28Taeniopygia_castanotis%29_pair.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Australian_zebra_finch_Chestnut-eared_Finch_%28Taeniopygia_castanotis%29_pair.jpg/500px-Australian_zebra_finch_Chestnut-eared_Finch_%28Taeniopygia_castanotis%29_pai